TI  - Primary Pleural Epitheliod Hemangioendothelioma with Lung Involvement.
PG  - S120-S121
AB  - The authors report a case of 50-year man who presented with 2-year history of dry 
      cough, right sided chest pain, and shortness of breath. Chest X-ray revealed 
      right-sided pleural effusion and left-sided opacity. Pleuroscopic pleural biopsy 
      confirmed the diagnosis of primary pleural epitheliod hemangioendothelioma (EHE) 
      with peripheral lung parenchymal invasion. Chest drain was inserted; and 
      significant amount of fluid was drained, but lung failed to expand after 72 
      hours. Patient was planned for video assisted thoracoscopy (VATS) and also 
      discussed with oncology department for chemotherapy; but he refused any further 
      treatment, and left home against medical advice with chest drain in place. EHE 
      originating from pleura is extremely rare with an aggressive clinical course and 
      poor prognosis. To our knowledge, this is the first reported case of an EHE 
      originating from pleura in South Asia and highlights the heterogeneous geographic 
      distribution of tumor and demonstrates the need for a more systemic approach to 
      all patients with unilateral pleural effusion.
