PMID- 42307772
OWN - NLM
STAT- MEDLINE
DCOM- 20260617
LR  - 20260629
IS  - 1948-8270 (Electronic)
IS  - 1948-8270 (Linking)
VI  - 33
IP  - 3
DP  - 2026 Jul 1
TI  - Optimal Number of Pleural Biopsies During Pleuroscopy (OPTIBIOP).
LID - e01080 [pii]
LID - 10.1097/LBR.0000000000001080 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: There is currently no evidence-based standard for the number of 
      pleural biopsies required during pleuroscopy to achieve diagnostic adequacy. This 
      study evaluated the cumulative diagnostic yield of sequential pleural biopsy 
      aliquots obtained during rigid pleuroscopy. METHODS: We conducted a prospective, 
      single-center observational pilot study of adult patients undergoing pleuroscopy. 
      Biopsy specimens were obtained according to routine practice and submitted in 
      sequential aliquots: aliquot 1 (biopsies 1 to 3), aliquot 2 (4 to 6), aliquot 3 
      (7 to 9), and aliquot 4 (≥10). Pathologic evaluation was performed sequentially 
      to determine the earliest aliquot yielding a definitive diagnosis. Cumulative 
      diagnostic yield, the need for additional aliquots for comprehensive biomarker 
      testing, and complications were evaluated. RESULTS: A definitive diagnosis was 
      achieved in all 104 patients. The median number of biopsies performed was 10 (IQR 
      6 to 14). The diagnostic yield for aliquot 1 (≤3 biopsies) was 93%. Among the 7% 
      of cases requiring subsequent aliquots for diagnosis, 6 had diffuse parietal 
      pleural abnormalities on pleuroscopy, while 1 had discrete lesions. Of the 17 
      patients who required comprehensive biomarker testing, 10 (59%) needed samples 
      beyond the first aliquot. No major complications were reported. CONCLUSION: While 
      most diagnoses can be established with 3 or fewer biopsies, cases requiring 
      additional biopsies were more frequently associated with diffuse pleural disease. 
      These findings support tailoring the biopsy strategy to pleural morphology: 
      lesion-directed sampling for focal disease and broader sampling for diffusely 
      abnormal or benign-appearing pleura, especially when malignancy is suspected, or 
      comprehensive biomarker testing is required.
