TI  - Disposable peel-away sheaths for pleural space access: a minimally invasive 
      conduit for medical thoracoscopy and chest tube insertion.
PG  - 7332-7342
LID - 10.21037/jtd-2025-870 [doi]
AB  - The global rise in lung diseases has renewed interest in thoracoscopy, 
      underscoring its growing role in modern clinical practice. As this field evolves, 
      the need for practical, adaptable, and cost-effective tools becomes increasingly 
      important. This report explores the use of peel-away sheaths to access the 
      pleural space during medical thoracoscopy, serving as conduits for both flexible 
      and rigid instruments. Their use enables precise placement of small-bore or 
      surgical chest tubes in targeted anatomical locations and allows for the 
      selection of sheath sizes based on procedural needs. Between April 2024 and 
      February 2025, an observational study was conducted at Los Angeles General 
      Medical Center, a tertiary care hospital, involving 45 consecutive patients 
      undergoing medical thoracoscopy using peel-away sheaths. The results demonstrated 
      a strong safety profile. There were no major complications, fatalities, or 
      instances of bleeding, lung injury, or insertion site infections. Patients 
      experienced minimal discomfort and favorable perioperative pain scores. The 
      technique proved versatile, functioning effectively across a range of anatomical 
      sites and levels of procedural complexity. It was also successfully implemented 
      in diverse clinical environments, including outpatient endoscopy suites and 
      inpatient step-down units. These findings highlight the practicality, safety, and 
      adaptability of peel-away sheaths in thoracoscopic procedures. Their ease of use, 
      compatibility with various instruments, and low cost make them especially 
      valuable in resource-conscious settings. By offering procedural flexibility and 
      improving access to the pleural space, this approach may support broader adoption 
      of medical thoracoscopy and enhance patient care across a variety of healthcare 
      settings.
