VI  - 67
IP  - 4
DP  - 2017 Apr
TI  - Simulation training using cadaver sheep chest in pleuroscopy - A step towards 
      skills enhancement.
PG  - 552-555
AB  - OBJECTIVE: For assessing the use of if simulation training on animal cadavers as 
      a useful tool for training in pleuroscopy. METHODS: The email-based 
      cross-sectional study was conducted in December 2014 at Shaikh Zayed Hospital, 
      Federal Postgraduate Medical Institute, Lahore, Pakistan, and comprised 
      respiratory physicians and trainees after their participation in a two-day 
      hands-on training course on pleuroscopy and pleural medical procedures. The 
      responses were analysed and the responses of physicians and trainees were 
      compared. RESULTS: Of the 44 individuals who attended the course and were 
      contacted through emails, 38(86.4%) responded, including 20(52.6%) physicians and 
      18(47.3%) trainees. All the 38(100%) subjects uniformly accepted the utility of 
      simulation training in enhancing education, improving skill, and improving 
      confidence by repeated practice, and felt that the inclusion of animal models for 
      learning fundamental pleuroscopic procedures can help a lot in teaching. 
      CONCLUSIONS: Animal cadavers can be used as an effective teaching tool for 
      pleuroscopy training.
