TI  - Pleural Cryobiopsy versus Flexible Forceps Biopsy in Subjects with Undiagnosed 
      Exudative Pleural Effusions Undergoing Semirigid Thoracoscopy: A Crossover 
      Randomized Trial (COFFEE Trial).
PG  - 133-141
LID - 10.1159/000497212 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: There is no randomized study comparing pleural cryobiopsy (CB) and 
      flexible forceps biopsy (FFB) in subjects undergoing medical thoracoscopy for the 
      diagnosis of pleural effusions. OBJECTIVE: In this crossover study, we compared 
      the diagnostic yield of CB versus FFB in subjects undergoing semirigid 
      thoracoscopy. METHODS: Subjects undergoing semirigid thoracoscopy for undiagnosed 
      pleural effusions were subjected to both CB and FFB, with the order of performing 
      the biopsy randomized in a 1:1 ratio. The primary outcome was the diagnostic 
      yield obtained with CB versus FFB. The secondary outcomes included the biopsy 
      size, depth, histologic interpretability, artefacts, the difficulty of performing 
      biopsy on an operator-rated visual analog scale, the severity of bleeding 
      observed at the time of the biopsy, and the duration of the procedure. RESULTS: 
      Of the 201 subjects screened, 50 (mean age 52.4 years; 18 women) were included. 
      The diagnostic yield of CB (78.0%) was not different from FFB (76.0%, p = 1.00). 
      CB yielded a larger specimen than FFB (median size 7.0 vs. 4.0 mm; p < 0.001), 
      and a greater depth of specimens (up to the pleural fat or deeper, CB vs. FFB 
      65.2 vs. 40.8%; p = 0.02). The CB procedure was quicker than FFB (median duration 
      10 vs. 15 min; p < 0.001). There were no significant differences in the 
      difficulty of performing the biopsy, the severity of bleeding, histologic 
      interpretability, or artefacts in the specimens between the CB and FFB groups. 
      CONCLUSION: The diagnostic yield of pleural CB was comparable to FFB during 
      semirigid thoracoscopy.
