DP  - 2026 Mar 31
TI  - Diagnostic evaluation of pleural effusion in patients with non-small lung cancer 
      affects time to diagnosis and treatment initiation.
PG  - 228
LID - 10.21037/jtd-2025-1-2527 [doi]
LID - 228
AB  - BACKGROUND: Malignant pleural effusion (MPE) is a common complication in patients 
      with non-small cell lung cancer and often signifies advanced disease. Timely 
      diagnosis of MPE is critical for initiating appropriate therapy; however, 
      cytologic analysis from thoracentesis has limited sensitivity, which often 
      results in diagnostic delays due to the need for repeat procedures. We sought to 
      identify factors associated with these delays. METHODS: We conducted a 
      multicenter retrospective cohort study of adult patients (≥18 years) with 
      presumed or confirmed non-small cell lung cancer and no radiographic evidence of 
      distant metastasis who underwent their first diagnostic intervention for a 
      pleural effusion (either thoracentesis or pleuroscopy). The primary outcome was 
      time from the initial diagnostic pleural intervention to initiation of cancer 
      treatment. Linear regression was used to evaluate associations between clinical 
      variables and diagnostic/treatment delays. RESULTS: A total of 136 patients were 
      included. Factors associated with delays from first pleural intervention to 
      treatment initiation included final pathologic stage, the 
      cytologic/histopathologic diagnosis at the first intervention, and number of 
      interventions. Patients who did not receive a diagnosis at the first intervention 
      experienced an average delay of 14.9 days compared with those diagnosed with 
      malignancy at the first intervention. Each additional intervention was associated 
      with a 19.4-day increase in time to treatment initiation. Similarly, time to 
      definitive diagnosis increased by 24.5 days per additional intervention. Patients 
      with a prior cancer history experienced an average delay of 13.9 days. Eastern 
      Cooperative Oncology Group (ECOG) scores 1 or 3 were associated with shorter 
      diagnostic delays compared with scores of 0 or 2. CONCLUSIONS: Diagnostic delays 
      in patients with non-small cell lung cancer and pleural effusion are 
      significantly influenced by the results of the initial intervention and the need 
      for repeat procedures. Earlier use of definitive diagnostic modalities such as 
      pleuroscopy may be associated with reduced time to diagnosis and treatment 
      initiation, particularly in patients with a high clinical suspicion of MPE.
